findings.body:
The light-theme check in `testCheckSystemThemeChanges` now reads `Appearance.darkThemeView`. After `Action.SelectLightTheme`, the dark button's value is "0", so `XCTAssertEqual(lightIsSelected as? String, "1")` fails on every run. The variable name `lightIsSelected` also no longer matches what is read.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/DisplaySettingsTests.swift" lines="46-55"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The PR description says a new test case was created to match the manual steps, but the diff edits the existing method in place. The TestRail ID also changes from 2337487 to 3298823. If the old case is still needed, this is a mis-mapping; if it is not, the description is wrong.

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/DisplaySettingsTests.swift" lines="53-54"}
        let lightIsSelected = app.buttons[AccessibilityIdentifiers.Settings.Appearance.lightThemeView].value
        XCTAssertEqual(lightIsSelected as? String, "1")

The `checkCopiedUrl()` call is removed from `testClipboard`, so the test now copies the URL and pastes it without checking that the clipboard value matches the address bar. The helper is now dead code, and its only remaining reference is a commented-out call in `testClipboardPasteAndGo` at line 102. The PR gives no reason for dropping the assertion. If the check is flaky on some iOS versions, it should be guarded in the test or moved, not removed.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/ClipBoardTests.swift" lines="66-73"}

::callout{type="info"}
`checkCopiedUrl` only runs its assertion when `#unavailable(iOS 16.0)` is true, so the original call was already a partial check. Dropping it removes coverage on iOS 15 and below.
